@@s001 Pattern matching
@@s002 ora:case I am trying,no:does not match,ok:matches
@@s003 The subject: List(1, 2, 3, 4, 5), that is five Cons cells. The cases are tried <b>from the top</b>, one at a time.
@@s004 Case 1: x = 1, then 2 = 2, then a 4 is needed in third position but there is a 3. <b>Does not match</b>.
@@s005 Case 2: the list is not empty. Does not match.
@@s006 Case 3: x = 1, y = 2, then 3 = 3, then 4 = 4; the rest (the cell of 5) goes into the <code>_</code>. <b>Matches</b>.
@@s007 The body of case 3 is run: x + y = 1 + 2 = <b>3</b>. Case 4 is not even tried: the first that matches wins.
@@s008 ora:case I am trying,no:does not match,ok:matches,dato:name created by the pattern
@@s009 ok:matches,no:does not match
@@s010 ok:surely covered,due:maybe (guard),no:compiler warning
@@s011 ora:heads h1 and h2,ok:result,no:ended
@@s012 no:wrong code and message,ok:correction
@@s013 Pattern matching
@@s014 <code>x match</code> asks "what shape does <code>x</code> have?". Each <code>case</code> describes a possible shape. Scala tries the <code>case</code>s from the top and chooses the <b>first</b> that matches, and meanwhile gives a name to the pieces you need.
@@s015 Reading a match
@@s016 head: the first element of a list
@@s017 <code>l</code> is the <b>subject</b>: the thing whose shape I ask about. <code>match</code> always goes <b>after</b> the subject.
@@s018 First case: if <code>l</code> is the empty list there is no first element. Here the exercise explicitly asks for an exception.
@@s019 Second case: <code>l</code> is a <code>Cons</code> cell.
@@s020 <code>h</code> <b>did not exist before</b>. By writing it in the pattern I say: "if it matches, call <code>h</code> the first field of the cell". The first field of <code>Cons</code> is the head.
@@s021 The second field (the tail) is there, but I do not need it: <code>_</code> means "here there is something, I discard it".
@@s022 To the right of the arrow the result of this case: the head.
@@s023 Frequent doubt: where does h come from?
@@s024 From the cell itself. <code>Cons(head: A, tail: List[A])</code> has two fields, in that order. The pattern <code>Cons(h, _)</code> lines them up with the names you choose: the first is called <code>h</code>, the second you throw away. You could write <code>Cons(primo, resto)</code> and it would be the same.
@@s025 head(List(7, 8)): the cases from the top
@@s026 A match run case by case
@@s027 Exercise 1 of week 2: what is this expression worth? You answer without running, trying the cases in order.
@@s028 Try the cases from the top
@@s029 First I write the list as cells: <code>List(1, 2, 3, 4, 5)</code> calls <code>List.apply</code>, which builds this.
@@s030 Case 1. <code>x</code> = 1, then 2 = 2 ok, then a 4 is needed in third position but there is a <b>3</b>. It does not match.
@@s031 Case 2. The list is not empty. It does not match.
@@s032 Case 3. <code>x</code> = 1, <code>y</code> = 2, then 3 = 3, then 4 = 4, the rest (<code>Cons(5, Nil)</code>) goes into the <code>_</code>. <b>It matches.</b>
@@s033 Result: <b>3</b> (checked). Cases 4 and 5 are not even looked at.
@@s034 The cases tried from the top
@@s035 With the course options
@@s036 Compiling this match with <code>-Werror</code> (the course's <code>project.scala</code>) Scala flags the last case: <i>Unreachable case except for null</i>. Every list is <code>Nil</code> or <code>Cons</code>, and <code>case Cons(h, t)</code> already takes them all: <code>case _ => 101</code> can never fire.
