@@s061 The rule of names
@@s062 In patterns a <b>lowercase</b> name is a new name, which gets filled. A <b>Capitalised</b> name is a thing that must already exist (a constructor such as <code>Nil</code> or a constant).
@@s063 The same value against six patterns
@@s064 Guards: <code>case … if</code>
@@s065 After the pattern you can add a condition. The case holds only if the pattern matches <b>and</b> the condition is true.
@@s066 The compiler does not count the conditions
@@s067 It matches only if it is a <code>Some</code> and <code>p(a)</code> is true.
@@s068 It seems to cover everything: <code>p(a)</code> is either true or false.
@@s069 For the compiler a guard is worth "maybe": it does not reason about what <code>p</code> does. So it thinks a <code>Some</code> could slip through.
@@s070 Solution 1: the last case without a guard.
@@s071 Solution 2: the decision goes inside the body.
@@s072 Why the compiler sees a "maybe"
@@s073 Matching on two lists together
@@s074 To go through two lists in parallel put the two lists in a pair and match on the pair (exercise 16).
@@s075 addPairwise
@@s076 The subject is the pair <code>(l, r)</code>. Each pattern will therefore have two pieces.
@@s077 If one of the two has ended, I stop. The <code>_</code> says "I am not interested in the other". Together they also cover <code>(Nil, Nil)</code>.
@@s078 Both have a cell: I take the two heads and the two tails, with different names.
@@s079 The result cell: sum of the heads, and as the rest the sum of the tails.
@@s080 The match on the pair (l, r)
